What is Git Stash?
Git stash temporarily saves your uncommitted changes.
It clears your working directory, letting you switch branches or pull updates safely.
Think of it as a locker where you store your work-in-progress until you’re ready to continue.

Why use Git Stash?
You’re in the middle of a feature but need to fix a production bug.
You want to pull the latest code but don’t want to commit unfinished work.
You want a clean workspace without losing progress.

Real-life example
Imagine you’re developing a discount feature for an e-commerce site on feature-discount:
You’ve made some changes but not finished yet.
Suddenly, a production bug needs fixing on main.
Instead of committing half-done work, you run:
git stash 
Common Stash Commands
1. Save changes to stash
 git stash
2. List all stashes
 git stash list
3. Reapply stashed changes
 git stash apply
4. Apply + remove stash
 git stash pop
5. Remove a stash
 git stash drop stash@{0}
6. Clear all stashes
 git stash clear

In Summary
Git stash is a temporary storage box for your changes — perfect for multitasking, fixing urgent bugs, or switching tasks without losing work.
